ng  people I think the way that we serve  people is by helping them go deeply into  what where it started right I mean I  meet people all the time you know who  come to the care farm to do work on  grief that happened when they were six  years old because that's when their  father shot himself and now they're 40  and it's affected his whole life without  even realizing even realizing it and and  and other people blaming you know him  for these maladaptive behaviors that  were coping mechanisms when you're six  and your dad shoots himself and the  whole family is spun out right and they  just become part of your habitual  patterns yes right and so I would I  would really have to take her deeper  into that and say let's talk about this  right because it's hard to self-motivate  when when you're being weighed down by  the unexplored past the the word that  stands out to me here is habits and I  like to delineate habits from routine  and of course she also says good habits  here which is important to recognize  because just because something is I have  it doesn't make it a good thing in fact  most of our habits tend to be bad habits  when when they aren't intentional right  when we have a risk right right when we  haven't been deliberate about about  bringing these new habits on so I think  if I were to rephrase her question maybe  the thing that I would say is how do I  keep up with a good routine because if  it is truly habitual than it is just a  habit you will continue to do it but  when trauma strikes or depression  strikes something like that it may throw  your routine off significantly and the  thing that has helped me in the past  when when dealing with with depression  or dealing with trauma is having those  habits to actually to fall back on and  when they are truly habitual you know  whether it's exercise or reading or  writing I tend to do them even you know  on the bad days so to speak and I know  that they actually the good habits help  me cope whereas the